---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import AnimatedBackground from "../../components/AnimatedBackground.astro";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="description" content="Sarah Whitaker's forest cover type machine learning project using R, feature engineering, and ExtraTrees." />
    <title>Forest Cover Type Prediction · sarahwhitaker</title>
  </head>
  <body class="night-design project-page">
    <AnimatedBackground />

    <main>
      <header class="project-hero">
        <a class="back-link" href="/">♡ back home</a>
        <p class="project-kicker">MACHINE LEARNING</p>
        <h1>Forest Cover Type Prediction</h1>
        <div class="floral-divider" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span><b>✿</b><span></span>
        </div>
        <p class="project-subtitle">R · tidymodels · feature engineering · ExtraTrees · classification</p>
      </header>

      <article class="data-content forest-content">
        <section class="data-hero">
          <p class="section-label">KAGGLE SCORE</p>
          <div class="big">0.79350</div>
          <h2>79.35% classification accuracy</h2>
          <p>
            In this Kaggle competition, the model predicts one of seven forest cover types
            from cartographic measurements. The score is an accuracy score, meaning the
            proportion of test-set predictions that matched the correct forest cover type.
            A score of <strong>0.79350</strong> means about 79 out of every 100 predictions
            were correct on the evaluated test data.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="section-label">01 · THE PROBLEM</p>
          <p>
            The Forest Cover Type dataset uses cartographic information such as elevation,
            aspect, slope, distances to hydrology, roads, and fire points, along with
            wilderness-area and soil-type indicators, to classify each observation into
            one of seven forest cover types.
          </p>
          <div class="cover-types">
            <span>Spruce / Fir</span>
            <span>Lodgepole Pine</span>
            <span>Ponderosa Pine</span>
            <span>Cottonwood / Willow</span>
            <span>Aspen</span>
            <span>Douglas-fir</span>
            <span>Krummholz</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="section-label">02 · FEATURE ENGINEERING</p>
          <p>
            I created additional variables from the distance measurements rather than
            relying only on the original predictors. These included differences and sums
            between distances to hydrology, roads, and fire points.
          </p>
          <div class="feature-grid">
            <div><b>Distance differences</b><span>hydrology − roads</span></div>
            <div><b>Distance differences</b><span>fire points − hydrology</span></div>
            <div><b>Distance differences</b><span>fire points − roads</span></div>
            <div><b>Distance sums</b><span>hydrology + roads</span></div>
            <div><b>Distance sums</b><span>fire points + hydrology</span></div>
            <div><b>Distance sums</b><span>fire points + roads</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="section-label">03 · MODELING</p>
          <div class="stats">
            <div><b>5-fold</b><span>cross-validation</span></div>
            <div><b>30</b><span>random hyperparameter combinations</span></div>
            <div><b>500</b><span>trees in final model</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            I used <strong>tidymodels</strong> to build a classification workflow and
            tuned an ExtraTrees-style random forest using the <strong>ranger</strong>
            engine. The tuning process searched over the number of predictors considered
            at each split and the minimum node size, using accuracy as the evaluation metric.
            The final model was refit with 500 trees before generating the Kaggle submission.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="section-label">04 · WHY THE SCORE MATTERS</p>
          <div class="score-explain">
            <div class="score-line"><span>0.00000</span><i>no predictions correct</i></div>
            <div class="score-line"><span>0.79350</span><i>my Kaggle result</i></div>
            <div class="score-line"><span>1.00000</span><i>every prediction correct</i></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Accuracy is calculated as the number of correct predictions divided by the
            total number of predictions. So the decimal is simply another way of writing
            a percentage: <strong>0.79350 = 79.35%</strong>. It does not mean the model
            is “79.35% confident”; it means roughly 79.35% of the evaluated predictions
            were correct.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="section-label">05 · TAKEAWAY</p>
          <p>
            This project gave me hands-on experience building a multiclass classification
            workflow from raw predictors through feature engineering, cross-validation,
            hyperparameter tuning, final model fitting, and submission. It also gave me
            practice translating a large set of measurements into features that a model
            could use to distinguish between forest cover types.
          </p>
          <div class="tags">
            <span>R</span>
            <span>tidymodels</span>
            <span>ranger</span>
            <span>ExtraTrees</span>
            <span>feature engineering</span>
            <span>cross-validation</span>
            <span>classification</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing-section">
          <p class="section-label">KAGGLE</p>
          <p>
            <a class="inline-project-link" href="https://www.kaggle.com/code/sarahwhitaker/forest-cover-type-model-and-predictions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">view the Kaggle notebook →</a>
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>

      <footer>
        <nav class="social-links" aria-label="Social links">
          <a href="https://github.com/sarahlouisewhitaker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GITHUB</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">♡</span>
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sarah-louise-whitaker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LINKEDIN</a>
        </nav>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
.forest-content{max-width:900px;margin:auto;padding:0 1rem 5rem}
.forest-content section{margin-bottom:4.5rem}
.data-hero{text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto 5rem}
.big{font-size:clamp(4rem,11vw,8rem);line-height:.9;color:var(--rose);text-shadow:0 0 25px rgba(239,180,202,.18)}
.data-hero h2{font-weight:500}
.forest-content section>p:not(.section-label){line-height:1.8;color:rgba(255,245,250,.82)}
.cover-types{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;gap:.55rem;margin-top:1.3rem}
.cover-types span,.tags span{padding:.55rem .8rem;border:1px solid rgba(239,180,202,.35);border-radius:99px;color:var(--rose);font-size:.72rem;background:rgba(8,10,22,.42)}
.feature-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:.8rem;margin-top:1.2rem}
.feature-grid div,.stats div,.score-line{background:rgba(8,10,22,.58);border:1px solid rgba(239,180,202,.25);border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem}
.feature-grid b,.stats b{display:block;color:var(--rose)}
.feature-grid span,.stats span{display:block;font-size:.78rem;opacity:.7;margin-top:.4rem}
.stats{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:.8rem;margin:1.2rem 0}
.stats b{font-size:1.35rem}
.score-explain{display:grid;gap:.65rem;margin:1.2rem 0}
.score-line{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem}
.score-line span{color:var(--rose);font-family:monospace}
.score-line i{font-size:.8rem;opacity:.7;font-style:normal}
.tags{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.6rem;margin-top:1.2rem}
@media(max-width:650px){
  .feature-grid,.stats{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .score-line{align-items:flex-start;flex-direction:column;gap:.35rem}
}
</style>
